description:
Fixes #4791

Not the best fix, but comment in code explains why:

```
        //TODO maybe should make `importToken` fail gracefully when requested for a server that isn't active instead, but it'd involve introducing optionals to other call-sites which seem to make things more complicated and worse
        guard sessions.hasKey(server) else { return }
```

Closes #3995

`NSPredicate(format:)` isn't type safe. Is there a better way? Does Realm provide some type-safe magic to form the predicate from the `Transaction` perhaps?

@oa-s would you help check the other `NSPredicate(format:)` calls for these? :)

Eliminate race condition in `useIsAddressValid`
## Summary:

In general, async code in React effects is unsafe. To be precise, _setting state_ after an awaitable is unsafe unless you track whether the effect that triggered you is still ‘relevant’ (ie. its dependencies haven't changed _and_ the component in which it lives has not unmounted).

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  (async () => {
    const result = await somethingThatTakesTime(foo);
    setResult(result); // `foo` might have changed by now!
  })();
}, [foo]);

// 1. `foo` gets set to `bar`
// 2. `somethingThatTakesTime` gets called for `bar`
// 3. `foo` changes to `baz`
// 4. `somethingThatTakesTime` gets called for `baz`
// 5. `somethingThatTakesTime` returns a result for `baz` and `setResult` gets called
// 6. `somethingThatTakesTime` returns a result for `bar` and `setResult` gets called
//
// Now `foo` is `baz`, but you're displaying the _result_ for `bar`. UI: corrupt.
```

In this PR we implement the tracking necessary to prevent this; we cancel in progress work whenever `address` changes. This will prevent a situation where the result from a _previous_ address ends up overwriting the result for the _current_ address.

## Test Plan:

Years of watching this bite developers in the ass.

I noticed intermittent failures in the `mempool_packages.py` RPC test; turns out I introduced a race condition in a recent pull (#7062).

Before this change, node1 could either be in sync or out of sync with node0 after `invalidateblock` was called, depending on how fast that happened.  The test would fail if they were out of sync.  This fixes the test by enforcing that their tips stay synced up.

@laanwj Note that this bug is in 0.12, in case you think this should be backported or included in the next 0.12 release candidate.

## Location

- **File**: `src/handler/sign_message.c`
- **Lines**: 85-145

## Classification

| Property | Value |
| --- | --- |
| **Severity** | High |
| **Category** | Other |
| **CWE** | CWE-CWE-457 |
| **OWASP** | A04:2021-Insecure Design |
| **Confidence** | Confirmed |
| **Likelihood** | Medium |

## Technical Description
When `message_length` is zero, `n_chunks` becomes 0 and the loop that fills `message_full` never executes. `message_full` is not initialized beforehand, and no explicit NUL terminator is written for the empty-message case. The code then treats `message_full` as a printable C string and passes it to the confirmation UI.

As a result, an empty SIGN_MESSAGE request causes the device to display whatever uninitialized stack bytes happen to be in `message_full` rather than an empty message. This leaks stack memory to the secure-screen UI and can misrepresent to the user what is being signed.

## Vulnerable Code

```c
85|    uint8_t message_full[MAX_DISPLAYBLE_MESSAGE_LENGTH + 1];
86|    size_t n_chunks = (message_length + MESSAGE_CHUNK_SIZE - 1) / MESSAGE_CHUNK_SIZE;
87|    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < n_chunks; i++) {
...
102|        if (i == n_chunks - 1) {
103|            // Last chunk - let's add null terminator at the end
104|            message_chunk[chunk_len] = '\0';
105|        }
...
133|#ifndef HAVE_AUTOAPPROVE_FOR_PERF_TESTS
134|    if (printable) {
135|        if (!ui_display_message_and_confirm(dc, path_str, (const char *) message_full, false)) {
136|            SEND_SW(dc, SW_DENY);
137|            return;
138|        }
139|    } else {
140|        if (!ui_display_message_and_confirm(dc, path_str, message_hash_str, true)) {
141|            SEND_SW(dc, SW_DENY);
142|            return;
143|        }
144|    }
145|#endif
```

## Impact
USB/host application: attacker controlling the connected wallet client can request signing of an empty message and cause the device to display uninitialized stack data during user confirmation. This can leak leftover memory on-screen and may trick the user into approving a signature while seeing unrelated content instead of the actual empty message.

## Remediation
Initialize `message_full` to zero before use and handle `message_length == 0` explicitly by setting `message_full[0] = '\0'` before the UI step.

---
*Created by [Cerberus](https://github.com/Donjon-Cerberus) Merlin*

## Location

- **File**: `src/plugins/erc20/erc20_plugin.c`
- **Lines**: 132-160

## Classification

| Property | Value |
| --- | --- |
| **Severity** | Critical |
| **Category** | Other |
| **CWE** | CWE-CWE-285 |
| **OWASP** | A01:2021-Broken Access Control |
| **Confidence** | Confirmed |
| **Likelihood** | Medium |

## Technical Description
The internal ERC20 plugin supports both `transfer(address,uint256)` and `approve(address,uint256)`, but the swap finalization path validates only the parsed address and amount. It never verifies that `context->selectorIndex` is `ERC20_TRANSFER`. During a swap, the Ethereum app later treats `G_swap_checked = true` as sufficient to auto-sign without showing the normal plugin review. Therefore an ERC20 `approve(spender, amount)` transaction whose spender and allowance match the swap destination/amount can satisfy the swap checks even though it grants an allowance instead of transferring tokens.

## Vulnerable Code

```c
            if (G_called_from_swap) {
                char buf[sizeof(strings.common.fullAmount)];
                const s_token_info *token_info;

                if (!getEthDisplayableAddress(context->destinationAddress,
                                              buf,
                                              sizeof(buf),
                                              g_chain_config->chain_id)) {
                    msg->result = ETH_PLUGIN_RESULT_ERROR;
                    break;
                }
                swap_check_destination(buf);

                uint64_t chain_id = get_tx_chain_id();
                if ((token_info = get_matching_token_info(&chain_id, msg->tokenLookup1)) == NULL) {
                    msg->result = ETH_PLUGIN_RESULT_ERROR;
                    break;
                }
                if (!amountToString(context->amount,
                                    sizeof(context->amount),
                                    token_info->decimals,
                                    token_info->ticker,
                                    buf,
                                    sizeof(buf))) {
                    msg->result = ETH_PLUGIN_RESULT_ERROR;
                    break;
                }
                swap_check_amount(buf);
                G_swap_checked = true;
            }
```

## Impact
APDU/user input via swap/caller app: a malicious host or compromised swap provider can present a token swap that the user validates as sending tokens to an address, but send the Ethereum app calldata for `approve(address,uint256)` with the same address and amount. The plugin sets `G_swap_checked = true`, allowing the transaction to be signed in the swap path. The attacker-controlled spender can then call `transferFrom` to take the approved tokens, turning the swap confirmation into an unintended token approval and potential token theft.

## Remediation
In swap mode, reject every ERC20 selector except `transfer(address,uint256)`. Perform this check before `swap_check_destination()`/`swap_check_amount()` and before setting `G_swap_checked`. Consider also enforcing that no extra calldata is present in swap mode, since standard ERC20 transfers do not require it.

---
*Created by [Cerberus](https://github.com/Donjon-Cerberus) Merlin*

