## Location

- **File**: `src/nbgl/ui_approve_tx.c`
- **Lines**: 214-230

## Classification

| Property | Value |
| --- | --- |
| **Severity** | High |
| **Category** | Other |
| **CWE** | CWE-CWE-787 |
| **OWASP** | A04:2021-Insecure Design |
| **Confidence** | Suspected |
| **Likelihood** | Medium |

## Technical Description
When the transaction UI is driven by an external plugin, the app trusts the plugin-reported screen counts (`numScreens` and `additionalScreens`) and stores the sum in `pluginUiMaxItems` without enforcing an upper bound. The review builder then accumulates that value in an 8-bit `nbPairs`, allocates `g_pairs` for the wrapped result, and later iterates over all `pluginUiMaxItems` items while populating the review list. A malicious or compromised external plugin can therefore trigger out-of-bounds writes in the transaction approval UI by returning an excessive number of screens.

## Vulnerable Code

```c
// src/features/sign_tx/logic_sign_tx.c
dataContext.tokenContext.pluginUiMaxItems =
    pluginFinalize.numScreens + pluginProvideInfo.additionalScreens;

// src/nbgl/ui_approve_tx.c
static uint8_t getNbPairs(bool displayNetwork, bool fromPlugin) {
    uint8_t nbPairs = 0;

    if (fromPlugin) {
        if ((pluginType != PLUGIN_TYPE_EXTERNAL) && (strings.common.fromAddress[0] != 0)) {
            nbPairs++;
        }
        // Count the plugin items
        nbPairs += dataContext.tokenContext.pluginUiMaxItems;
        if (displayNetwork) {
            nbPairs++;
        }
        // Count the Max fees
        nbPairs++;
    }
    return nbPairs;
}

for (pairIndex = 0; pairIndex < dataContext.tokenContext.pluginUiMaxItems; pairIndex++) {
    ...
    g_pairs[nbPairs].item = plugin_buffers[counter].title;
    g_pairs[nbPairs].value = plugin_buffers[counter].msg;
    nbPairs++;
}
```

## Impact
Internal-only: a malicious installed external plugin application can return a crafted high screen count to the Ethereum app, corrupt memory during transaction review construction, and crash the app or potentially gain code execution on an embedded target with weak exploit mitigations. The blast radius includes any transaction flow delegated to an external plugin.

## Remediation
Reject plugin UIs whose total number of requested screens exceeds a conservative maximum, and use a wider integer type for intermediate pair counting before down-casting only after validation. Also bound-check the population loop against `g_pairsList->nbPairs`.

---
*Created by [Cerberus](https://github.com/Donjon-Cerberus) Merlin*

## Location

- **File**: `src/nbgl/ui_gcs.c`
- **Lines**: 401-443

## Classification

| Property | Value |
| --- | --- |
| **Severity** | High |
| **Category** | Other |
| **CWE** | CWE-CWE-190 |
| **OWASP** | A04:2021-Insecure Design |
| **Confidence** | Suspected |
| **Likelihood** | Medium |

## Technical Description
`ui_gcs()` stores both the computed pair count and the current output index in `uint8_t` variables even though the underlying field table size is returned as `size_t` and there is no visible upper bound on the number of GCS fields accepted from the host. A remote sender can submit more than 255 formatted fields via the generic clear-signing APDU sequence; `nbPairs += field_table_size()` then wraps, `ui_pairs_init()` allocates too few entries, and the later loop still iterates over the full field-table size while writing through `g_pairs[pair]`. The `pair` index itself is also `uint8_t`, so it can wrap during population.

This creates attacker-controlled out-of-bounds writes while building the confirmation UI for generic clear-signing transactions.

## Vulnerable Code

```c
    uint8_t nbPairs = 0;
    uint8_t pair = 0;
    uint8_t tx_idx = 0;
    const s_tx_info *info_tx = get_current_tx_info();
...
    // Contract info
    nbPairs += 1;
    // Batch transactions
    if (txContext.batch_nb_tx > 1) {
        nbPairs += txContext.batch_nb_tx;  // one page per sub-tx
    }
    // TX fields
    nbPairs += field_table_size();
    show_network = get_tx_chain_id() != chainConfig->chainId;
    if (show_network) {
        nbPairs += 1;
    }
    // Fees
    nbPairs += 1;

    if (!ui_pairs_init(nbPairs)) {
        return false;
    }
```

## Impact
Network: a remote host using the generic clear-signing flow (`INS_GTP_FIELD` / `INS_SIGN` start-flow) can supply enough fields to overflow the 8-bit `nbPairs`/`pair` counters and trigger heap corruption during UI construction. On Ledger devices this can crash the app or corrupt security-critical review data shown to the user before approval.

## Remediation
Track pair counts and indexes with `size_t` or at least `uint16_t`, and reject oversized field tables before allocating. Add an explicit maximum supported number of UI pairs and fail closed when the field table exceeds it.

---
*Created by [Cerberus](https://github.com/Donjon-Cerberus) Merlin*

### ✅ Checklist

- [x] \`npx changeset\` was attached.
- [ ] **Covered by automatic tests.** _The crash reproduced at runtime when the feature flag is enabled; existing integration tests are blocked by a pre-existing \`marketApi\` issue unrelated to this change._
- [x] **Impact of the changes:**
  - ModularDrawer opening under `lwmWallet40` feature flag (Lumen BottomSheet path)
  - Asset selection screen: pressing the search input while assets are loading
  - Network and Account selection screens: visual header rendering under the feature flag

### 📝 Description

The `QueuedDrawerBottomSheet` (Lumen-based drawer) was developed as a migration target for `QueuedDrawerGorhom`, gated behind the `lwmWallet40` feature flag. The feature had been disabled with a `// TODO: Re-enable it for LIVE-27294` comment after a crash was discovered.

**Problem (LIVE-27193):** When the Lumen BottomSheet was active and the user pressed the search input in the asset selection screen before assets had finished loading, `snapToIndex(1)` was called unconditionally — crashing when the list was empty. The root cause was in `expandToFullHeight`:

```ts
// Before — crash when assets not yet loaded
const expandToFullHeight = () => {
  snapToIndex(1); // ← always called, even on empty list
  if (formattedAssets.length > 0) {
    listRef.current?.scrollToIndex({ index: 0 });
  }
};

// After — safe guard covers both calls
const expandToFullHeight = () => {
  if (formattedAssets.length > 0) {
    snapToIndex(1);
    listRef.current?.scrollToIndex({ index: 0 });
  }
};
```

**Fix (LIVE-27294):**
1. Re-enabled the `lwmWallet40` feature flag gating in `ModularDrawer.tsx` and `ModularDrawerFlowView.tsx`.
2. Fixed `expandToFullHeight` in `AssetSelection` to guard both `snapToIndex` and `scrollToIndex` on a non-empty list.

### ❓ Context

- **JIRA**: [LIVE-27294](https://ledgerhq.atlassian.net/browse/LIVE-27294) — Fix crash & re-enable Lumen BottomSheet
- **JIRA**: [LIVE-27316](https://ledgerhq.atlassian.net/browse/LIVE-27316)
- **Original crash report**: [LIVE-27193](https://ledgerhq.atlassian.net/browse/LIVE-27193)

---

