INS_SET_PLUGIN carries a backend-signed binary payload that registers
a plugin for the next transaction. A regression here lets a hostile
host register an arbitrary plugin alias for any (contract, selector)
tuple — the device would then display the matching plugin UI for
whatever bytes the user actually signs.

Drive handle_set_plugin end-to-end and pin every validation gate:
- happy path with ERC721 and ERC1155 (PROD key allowed for these);
- header too small / TYPE / VERSION rejected at the front guards;
- declared payload size > actual dataLength rejected;
- plugin name longer than PLUGIN_ID_LENGTH (30) rejected (defense
  against pluginName buffer overflow);
- incompatible chain_id rejected via app_compatible_with_chain_id;
- wrong KEY_ID (TEST instead of PROD without HAVE_NFT_STAGING_KEY)
  rejected;
- wrong ALGORITHM_ID (anything other than SECG_P256K1+SHA256)
  rejected;
- signature length below MIN_DER_SIG_SIZE (67) / above
  MAX_DER_SIG_SIZE (72) rejected — pins the ASN.1 DER bounds;
- declared sig_len exceeds remaining dataLength rejected;
- check_signature_with_pubkey failure rejects;
- PROD key with an external plugin name (not ERC721/ERC1155)
  rejected — the load-bearing restriction that prevents the
  production signing key from being repurposed to sign arbitrary
  external plugin registrations;
- set_swap_with_calldata_plugin_type() flips pluginType.

